\title{ \textbf{Analytical soliton solutions of the ill-possed Boussinesq equation via a unified approach}}
\author{ Ali Akg\"{u}l$^{1,2,*}$, Farrah Ashraf $^{3}$, Rehan Khursheed $^{4}$, Romana Ashraf $^{5}$ \\
$^{1}$ Department of Electronics and Communication Engineering,\\ Saveetha School of Engineering,
SIMATS, Chennai, Tamilnadu, India.\\
$^{2}$ Siirt University, Art and Science Faculty, Department of Mathematics, 56100 Siirt, Türkiye.\\
$^{3}$ Department of Computer Science $ \& $ IT,\\ The University of Lahore, Lahore, Pakistan.\\
$^{4}$ Department of Mathematics and Statistics,\\ The University of Lahore, Lahore, Pakistan.\\
$^{3}$ Department of Computer Science $ \& $ IT,\\ The University of Lahore, Lahore, Pakistan.\\
Corresponding (*): aliakgul00727@gmail.com\\
e-mail: \{f.as70000, rehankhursheed468, dr.romanauetian\}@gmail.com }
\date{ }
\maketitle
% !TeX spellcheck = en_US
\begin{abstract}
\noindent
In this paper, Unified approach method is proposed to construct more general precise (exact) solutions of ill-possed Boussinesq equation. This technique can be used to obtain the soliton solutions for the non-linear partial differential equation. This method can facilitate a deeper understanding of the wave phenomenon and also demonstrate the significance of researching different non-linear waves that occur in ocean engineering and physics. This technique has successfully provided several new solitons for ill-possed Boussinesq equation like Dark soliton, Kink shaped soliton, Periodic soliton and Dispersion-less soliton. Appropriate values are given to the parameters involved in the solutions of the equation for graphical view of our results. Our results show the efficiency and favor-ability of unified approach method for solving other non-linear partial differential equations.
\end{abstract}
\textbf{Key words}:Unified approach method, Analytical soliton solutions, ill-posed Boussinesq equation, Kink shaped soliton, Singular soliton and Periodic soliton solutions.
\section{Introduction}
\noindent One of the most helpful subject in the literature is the study of accurate solutions to equations (differential). It is also crucial for explanation of many ambiguous subjects in applied science and engineering. The Boussinesq approximation is applied to water waves in fluid dynamics. It is an approximation that works for waves that are long and weakly non-linear. The approximation bears Joseph Boussinesq's name who derived it initially in response to the observation of the translational wave by  John Scott Russell. The Boussinesq equations were first presented in a publication by Boussinesq in 1872. In contrast to the equations for shallow water, the Boussinesq equations account for frequency dispersion. These equations take into account how wave frequency affects wave behavior that the shallow water equations do not account for. The solitons obtained from Boussinesq equations can either create a singularity in a finite amount of time or decay when perturbed which means that they gradually diminishes in stability when disrupted. It is likely that one would consider the Boussinesq equation to be very unique in and of itself. But in-fact, it is a physiologically meaningful equation that represents a non-linear integrable generalization of the wave equation. With the KdV equation being the most basic it is also a typical illustration of dimensional reduction within the context of the KP hierarchy. Many characteristics in fluid systems, signal processing, non-linear systems, plasma physics, fluid dynamics, electricity, science and engineering are modeled by non-linear partial equations.\\\
The Boussinesq equation is a fundamental generalized wave equation that arises in fluid mechanics and plasma physics and describes the propagation of small amplitude long waves in shallow water is viewed as
\begin{eqnarray}
u_{tt}-u_{xx}-\beta(u^2)_{xx}-\gamma u_{xxxx}=0,
\end{eqnarray}
where $\beta$ is the non-linear parameter representing the depth of the fluid, $\gamma$ is the characteristic speed of the long wave and \emph{u (x, t)} is the elevation of the free surface of the fluid. \emph{x} and \emph{t} are the scaled spatial and temporal variables respectively.\\\
Finding accurate solutions to partial differential equations is a significant research in the context of science and engineering. Different methods can be adopted to find the accurate solution to the non-linear partial differential equations. Well-known techniques for employing computational models and programs like Maple to find optimal solutions to non-physical problems have been applied to recent work in the past several years. These techniques or methods are as follows: Method of Homotopy perturbation \cite{A1}, Method of variational iteration \cite{A2}, Method of The $G/G^{'}$ - Expansion \cite{A3,a4,a5}, The method of Exp-Function \cite{a6,a7,a8,a9}, Method of Modified simple equation \cite{a10,a11}, Method of tanh function \cite{a12,a13,a14}, Method of Kudryashov \cite{a15,a16}, Method of The Trial equation \cite{a17}, Method of the Backscatter \cite{a18}, Backlund transform\cite{a19}, Method of Hirota bilinear \cite{a20}, Homogeneous balance method\cite{a21} and Method of Jacobi and Weierstrass elliptic function \cite{a22} etc. One of the finest ways to get the precise answers to the (NLPDE's) non-linear partial differential equations is to use the Unified Approach Technique. This approach will be used in this study to show how the ill-posed Boussinesq problem behaves and to determine its precise solution. Numerous precise solutions to the ill-posed Boussinesq problem can be found using this method with different shapes of solitons like Periodic, Kink, Singular, Dispersion-less and Dark solitons.
\section{Unified Approach Method }
The methodology of unified approach method has the following steps:
\subsection{Different Steps of the Unified Approach method}
\textbf{Step 1:}
We use the traveling wave transformation equation to convert
\begin{eqnarray}\label{PDE}
\Omega(u_x,u_t,u_{xt},u_{xx},u_{xxx}........)=0,
\end{eqnarray}
into ODE form (By combining the variables we get)
\begin{eqnarray}\label{ODE}
\Pi(U',U'',U''',.............)=0.
\end{eqnarray}\\\
\textbf{Step 2:}
 We now examine the solution of Eq. \ref{ODE} which takes the following form
\begin{eqnarray}\label{TF}
\frac{d}{d \zeta}\eta \! \left(\zeta \right) =H_{0}+ \sum_{j=1}^M H_{j}\eta \left(\zeta \right)^{j}+\sum_{j=1}^M \frac{F_{j}}{\eta \left(\zeta \right)^{j}}.
\end{eqnarray}
\textbf{Step 3:}
We may now calculate the value of M by balancing the highest order derivative term in Eq. \ref{ODE} with the highest order non-linear term.\\\
Reccati Equation of the form:
\begin{eqnarray}\label{RE}
\frac{d}{d \zeta}\eta \! \left(\zeta \right) =\eta \! \left(\zeta \right)^{2}+N.
\end{eqnarray}
Here we have different families ${\eta(\zeta)}$ of Eq. \ref{RE} depending upon the value of \emph{N} (The value of \emph{N} can be Zero, negative and positive). \\\
\textbf{when $\textbf{ N $<$ 0} $ }
\begin{align}\label{FAM1}
\begin{cases}\\\
\textbf{(i)}\quad \eta \! \left(\zeta \right) =\frac{-\sqrt{-\left(P^{2}+Q^{2}\right) N}-P \sqrt{-N}\, \cosh \, \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(\zeta +\psi \right)\right)}{P \sinh \, \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(\zeta +\psi \right)\right)+Q},\\\
\textbf{(ii)}\quad \eta \! \left(\zeta \right) =\frac{\sqrt{-\left(P^{2}+Q^{2}\right) N}-P \sqrt{-N}\, \cosh \, \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(\zeta +\psi \right)\right)}{P \sinh \, \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(\zeta +\psi \right)\right)+Q},\\\
\textbf{(iii)}\quad \eta \! \left(\zeta \right) =-\sqrt{-N}+\frac{2 P \sqrt{-N}}{P +\cosh \, \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(\zeta +\psi \right)\right)-\sinh \, \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(X +\psi \right)\right)},\\\
\textbf{(iv)}\quad \eta \! \left(\zeta \right) =\sqrt{-N}+\frac{2 P \sqrt{-N}}{P +\cosh \, \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(\zeta +\psi \right)\right)-\sinh \, \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(X +\psi \right)\right)}.\\\
\end{cases}
\end{align}
\textbf{when $\textbf{ N $>$ 0} $ }
\begin{align}\label{FAM2}
\begin{cases}\\\
\textbf{(i)}\quad \eta \! \left(\zeta \right) =\frac{-\sqrt{\left(P^{2}+Q^{2}\right) N}-P \sqrt{N}\, \cos \, \left(2 \sqrt{N}\, \left(\zeta +\psi \right)\right)}{P \sin \, \left(2 \sqrt{N}\, \left(\zeta +\psi \right)\right)+Q},\\\
\textbf{(ii)}\quad \eta \! \left(\zeta \right) =\frac{\sqrt{\left(P^{2}+Q^{2}\right) N}-P \sqrt{N}\, \cos \, \left(2 \sqrt{N}\, \left(\zeta +\psi \right)\right)}{P \sin \, \left(2 \sqrt{N}\, \left(\zeta +\psi \right)\right)+Q},\\\
\textbf{(iii)}\quad \eta \! \left(\zeta \right) =-i \sqrt{N}+\frac{2 i P \sqrt{N}}{P +\cos \, \left(2 \sqrt{N}\, \left(\zeta +\psi \right)\right)-i \sin \, \left(2 \sqrt{N}\, \left(\zeta +\psi \right)\right)},\\\
\textbf{(iv)}\quad \eta \! \left(\zeta \right) =i \sqrt{-N}+\frac{2 i P \sqrt{N}}{P +\cos \, \left(2 \sqrt{N}\, \left(\zeta +\psi \right)\right)-i \sin \, \left(2 \sqrt{N}\, \left(X +\psi \right)\right)}.\\\
\end{cases}
\end{align}
\textbf{when $\textbf{ N $=$ 0}$ }
\begin{eqnarray}\label{FAM3}
\quad \eta \! \left(\zeta \right) = -\frac{1}{\zeta +\psi}.
\end{eqnarray}
\section{Solving the ill-posed Boussinesq Equation}

Non-linear ill-posed Boussinesq equation of the form
\begin{eqnarray}\label{PDE1}
u_{\mathit{tt}} =u_{\mathit{xx}}+\left(u^{2}\right)_{\mathit{xx}}+u_{\mathit{xxxx}},
\end{eqnarray}
Using the transformation
\begin{eqnarray}\label{TF1}
u(x,t)=U(\zeta), \qquad \zeta=x - v t.
\end{eqnarray}
in Eq. \ref{PDE1} and integrating the result twice we get our required ODE form of ill-posed Boussinesq equation
\begin{eqnarray}\label{ODE1}
\left(v^{2}-1\right) U -U^{2}-U'' = 0.
\end{eqnarray}
We now obtain \emph{M = 2} by applying the Homogeneous Balancing Principle, which involves balancing the maximum degree non-linear term $U^{2}$ with the maximum order derivative term $U_{\zeta \zeta}$.\\\\
Now by replacing $M=2$ in equation in Eq. \ref{TF} we get
\begin{eqnarray}\label{S}
U  \left(\zeta \right) =H_{0}+H_{1} \eta  \left(\zeta \right)+H_{2} \eta  \left(\zeta \right)^{2}+\frac{F_{1}}{\eta \! \left(\zeta \right)}+\frac{F_{2}}{\eta  \left(\zeta \right)^{2}}.
\end{eqnarray}
here we need to find the constants involved in $U{(\zeta)}$ which are $H_{0}$, $H_{1}$, $H_{2}$, $F_{1}$ and $F_{2}$,
The $\eta{(\zeta)}$ represents (the families given) the solutions of Eq. \ref{RE}.\\
Now substitute Eq. \ref{RE} and Eq. \ref{S} in Eq. \ref{ODE1} then simplify resulting equation to get the coefficients of all powers of ${\eta(\zeta)}$ and set all the coefficients of the various powers of $\eta{(\zeta)}$ to zero to produce a set of simultaneous solvable algebraic equations.
The coefficients of different powers of $\eta{(\zeta)}$ are as follows\\
\begin{align}
\begin{cases}
\eta{(\zeta)}^{-4} :\;\;\;\quad -2 F_{1} N^{2}-2 F_{1} F_{2}=0, \\\
\eta{(\zeta)}^{-3} :\;\,\quad -2 F_{1} N^{2}-2 F_{1} F_{2}=0, \\\
\eta{(\zeta)}^{-2} :\;\,\quad F_{2} v^{2}-8 F_{2} N -F_{1}^{2}-2 H_{0} F_{2}-F_{2}=0,\\\
\eta{(\zeta)}^{-1} :\,\;\quad v^{2} F_{1}-2 N F_{1}-2 F_{1} H_{0}-2 F_{2} H_{1}-F_{1}=0, \\\
\eta{(\zeta)}^{0}  :\qquad -2 H_{2} N^{2}+H_{0} v^{2}-2 H_{1} F_{1}-2 H_{2} F_{2}-H_{0}^{2}-2 F_{2}-H_{0}=0,\\\
\eta{(\zeta)}^{1}  :\qquad H_{1} v^{2}-2 H_{1} N -2 H_{2} F_{1}-2 H_{0} H_{1}-H_{1}=0, \\\
\eta{(\zeta)}^{2}  :\qquad v^{2} H_{2}-8 N H_{2}-2 H_{0} H_{2}-H_{1}^{2}-H_{2}=0, \\\
\eta{(\zeta)}^{3}  :\qquad-2 H_{1} H_{2}-2 H_{1}=0, \\\
\eta{(\zeta)}^{4}  :\qquad-H_{2}^{2}-6 H_{2}=0.\\\
\end{cases}
\end{align}
Now by using Maple software we find the simultaneous solution of these equations.\\\
\textbf{Set I:}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{set1}
v = \sqrt{16 N -1},\,F_{1} = 0,\,F_{2} = -6 N^{2},\, H_{0} = -12 N,\, H_{1} = 0\,{\&}\,H_{2} = -6.
\end{eqnarray}
\textbf{Solution of Set I:}\\
By substituting Eq. \ref{TF1}, Eq. \ref{set1} and using \textbf{(iv)} from Eq. \ref{FAM1}, \textbf{(v,vii)} from Eq. \ref{FAM2} and Eq. \ref{FAM3} in Eq. \ref{S} we will get the following solutions.
\begin{eqnarray}\label{U1}
U_{1} \left(\zeta \right) =H_{0}+\frac{H_{2} \left[\sqrt{\left(P^{2}+Q^{2}\right) N}-P \sqrt{N}\, \cos \! \left(2 \sqrt{N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)\right]}{P \sin \! \left(2 \sqrt{N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)+Q}+\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{F_{2} \left[P \sin \! \left(2 \sqrt{N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)+Q \right]^{2}}{{\left[\sqrt{\left(P^{2}+Q^{2}\right) N}-P \sqrt{N}\, \cos \! \left(2 \sqrt{N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)\right]}^{2}}.
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{U2}
U_{2} \left(\zeta \right) =
H_{0}+\frac{H_{2} \left[-\sqrt{\left(P^{2}+Q^{2}\right) N}-P \sqrt{N}\, \cos \! \left(2 \sqrt{N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)\right]}{P \sin \! \left(2 \sqrt{N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)+Q}+\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{F_{2} \left[P \sin \! \left(2 \sqrt{N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)+Q \right]^{2}}{{\left[-\sqrt{\left(P^{2}+Q^{2}\right) N}-P \sqrt{N}\, \cos \! \left(2 \sqrt{N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)\right]}^{2}}.
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{U3}
U_{3} \left(\zeta \right) =
H_{0}+H_{2} \left[\sqrt{-N}+\frac{2 P \sqrt{-N}}{P +\cosh \! \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)-\sinh \! \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)}\right]+\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{F_{2}}{\left[\sqrt{-N}+\frac{2 P \sqrt{-N}}{P +\cosh \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)-\sinh \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)}\right]^{2}}.
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{U4}
U_{4}\left(\zeta \right) =H_{0}-\frac{H_{2}}{-v t +\psi +x}+F_{2} \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)^{2}.
\end{eqnarray}
\textbf{Set II:}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{set2}
v = \sqrt{-4 N -1},\,F_{1} = 0,\,F_{2} = -6 N^{2},\, H_{0} = -2 N,\, H_{1} = 0\,{\&}\, H_{2} = 0.
\end{eqnarray}
By substituting Eq. \ref{TF1}, Eq. \ref{set2} and using Eq. \ref{FAM1}, \ref{FAM3} in Eq. \ref{S} we will get the following solutions.\\\
\textbf{Solution of Set II:}\\\
\begin{eqnarray}\label{U11}
U_{1}\! \left(\zeta \right) =H_{0}+\frac{F_{2} \left[P \sinh \! \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)+Q \right]}{\sqrt{-\left(P^{2}+Q^{2}\right) N}-P \sqrt{-N}\, \cosh \! \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)}.
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{U22}
U_{2}\! \left(\zeta \right) =H_{0}+\frac{F_{2} \left[P \sinh \! \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)+Q \right]}{-\sqrt{-\left(P^{2}+Q^{2}\right) N}-P \sqrt{-N}\, \cosh \! \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)}.
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{U33}
U_{3}\! \left(\zeta \right) =H_{0}+\frac{F_{2}}{\sqrt{-N}+\frac{2 P \sqrt{-N}}{P +\cosh \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)-\sinh \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)}}.
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{U44}
U_{4}\! \left(\zeta \right) =H_{0}+\frac{F_{2}}{-\sqrt{-N}+\frac{2 P \sqrt{-N}}{P +\cosh \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)-\sinh \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-v t +\psi +x \right)\right)}}.
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{U55}
U_{5}\! \left(\zeta \right) = H_{0}+F_{2} \left(v t -\psi -x \right).
\end{eqnarray}
\textbf{Set III:}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{set3}
v = \sqrt{-16 N -1},\,F_{1} = 0,\,F_{2} = -6 N^{2},\, H_{0} = 4 N,\, H_{1} = 0,\,{\&}\, H_{2} = -6.
\end{eqnarray}
\textbf{Solution of Set III:}\\
By substituting Eq. \ref{TF1}, Eq. \ref{set3} and using Eq. \ref{FAM1}, \ref{FAM3} in Eq. \ref{S} we will get the following solutions.\\
\begin{eqnarray}\label{U111}
U_{1}\ \left(\zeta \right) = H_{0}+H_{2} \left[\sqrt{-N}+\frac{2 P \sqrt{-N}}{P +\cosh \! \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\,
\left(-t v +\psi +x \right)\right)-\sinh \! \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-t v +\psi +x \right)\right)}\right]^{2}+\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{F_{2}}{\left[\sqrt{-N}+\frac{2 P \sqrt{-N}}{P +\cosh \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-t v +\psi +x \right)\right)-\sinh \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\,
\left(-t v +\psi +x \right)\right)}\right]^{2}}.
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{U222}
U_{2}\! \left(\zeta \right) =
H_{0}+H_{2} \left[-\sqrt{-N}+\frac{2 P \sqrt{-N}}{P +\cosh \! \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-t v +\psi +x \right)\right)-\sinh \! \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-t v +\psi +x \right)\right)}\right]^{2}+\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{F_{2}}{\left[-\sqrt{-N}+\frac{2 P \sqrt{-N}}{P +\cosh \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-t v +\psi +x \right)\right)-\sinh \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-t v +\psi +x \right)\right)}\right]^{2}}.
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{U333}
U_{3}\! \left(\zeta \right) =
H_{0}+\frac{H_{2} {\left[\sqrt{-\left(P^{2}+Q^{2}\right) N}-P \sqrt{-N}\, \cosh \! \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-t v +\psi +x \right)\right)\right]}^{2}}{\left[P \sinh \! \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-t v +\psi +x \right)\right)+Q \right]^{2}}+\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{F_{2} \left[P \sinh \! \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-t v +\psi +x \right)\right)+Q \right]^{2}}{{\left[\sqrt{-\left(P^{2}+Q^{2}\right) N}-P \sqrt{-N}\, \cosh \! \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-t v +\psi +x \right)\right)\right]}^{2}}.
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{U444}
U_{4}\! \left(\zeta \right) =
H_{0}+\frac{H_{2} {\left[-\sqrt{-\left(P^{2}+Q^{2}\right) N}-P \sqrt{-N}\, \cosh \! \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-t v +\psi +x \right)\right)\right]}^{2}}{\left[P \sinh \! \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-t v +\psi +x \right)\right)+Q \right]^{2}}+\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{F_{2} \left[P \sinh \! \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-t v +\psi +x \right)\right)+Q \right]^{2}}{{\left[-\sqrt{-\left(P^{2}+Q^{2}\right) N}-P \sqrt{-N}\, \cosh \! \left(2 \sqrt{-N}\, \left(-t v +\psi +x \right)\right)\right]}^{2}}.
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{U555}
U_{5}\! \left(\zeta \right) =
H_{0}+\frac{H_{2}}{\left(-t v +\psi +x \right)^{2}}+F_{2} \left(-t v +\psi +x \right)^{2}.
\end{eqnarray}
